---
import Ironside from "../layouts/Ironside.astro";
import QuoteForm from "../components/QuoteForm.astro";
import CityFaq from "../components/CityFaq.astro";
import CityServiceSchema from "../components/CityServiceSchema.astro";
import ServiceAreaMap from "../components/ServiceAreaMap.astro";
import { cityBySlug, siblingsOf } from "../data/cities";

const city = cityBySlug("vaughan")!;
const nearby = siblingsOf("vaughan");

const title = "Epoxy flooring Vaughan";
const description =
 "Epoxy flooring Vaughan: Kleinburg, Woodbridge, Vellore Village and Patterson. Metallic and flake finishes, diamond-ground prep.";

const faqs = [
 {
 q: "I want a showroom finish in a heated garage in Kleinburg. What system is that?",
 a: "That is metallic epoxy, and Vaughan is where we install most of it. It is a hand-worked decorative system - metallic pigment dispersed into a clear resin, moved with a trowel and a blower to create depth and movement, then sealed. No two floors come out identical, which is the point. It belongs in a heated, finished, largely dry garage. In a working garage that sees a salt-covered car every day it will look tired quickly, because a mirror finish shows everything.",
 },
 {
 q: "How is metallic different from the flake floors I see everywhere?",
 a: "Flake is a broadcast system: you throw vinyl chips into a wet basecoat to refusal, scrape it back, and seal it. It is hard-wearing, hides dirt and tyre marks well, and it is what most garages should have. Metallic is a poured decorative finish with no chips - smoother, glossier, far more dramatic, and less forgiving of dust and scuffs. Flake is the working floor; metallic is the feature floor. Plenty of Vaughan clients put metallic in a display bay and flake in the working bays of the same garage.",
 },
 {
 q: "My garage in Patterson has four bays and in-floor heat. Any complications?",
 a: "In-floor heat needs managing rather than avoiding. The system has to be turned down or off ahead of the install and brought back gradually afterwards, because coating onto a warm slab shortens an already-short working window and can cause pinholing as air moves out of the concrete. We will ask you to drop it a couple of days in advance. Four bays is a full-day job with a larger crew - the area is the constraint, not the heating.",
 },
 {
 q: "Do you cover Woodbridge and the older parts of Vaughan?",
 a: "Yes. Woodbridge has the widest span of housing ages in the city, from 1970s stock through to recent infill, and the older garages there are often meticulously maintained - a lot of them have been swept daily for thirty years. That is genuinely helpful: a clean, uncontaminated slab grinds beautifully. The one thing we look for in older Woodbridge garages is a previous coating, since a fair number were done years ago and a failing legacy coating has to come off before we can bond to anything.",
 },
 {
 q: "How long does a coating last in a garage that barely gets used?",
 a: "Longer than the warranty conversation implies, but wear is not the only thing that ages a floor. UV is the other. A lightly used garage with a west-facing bay door that sits open on summer afternoons will amber a standard epoxy topcoat within a season or two. An aliphatic polyaspartic topcoat is UV-stable and will not. If your garage is more display space than workspace, that is the specification detail that actually matters to you.",
 },
 {
 q: "Vaughan is the far side of the GTA from Oakville. Does that affect scheduling?",
 a: "It is our longest regular run - roughly fifty minutes on the 407 - so Vaughan jobs are booked as dedicated days rather than slotted alongside a Halton job. In practice that is better for you, not worse: the crew arrives with the whole day allocated to your floor. We do ask for a firm confirmation a few days out, because a cancelled Vaughan day is harder to backfill than a cancelled Mississauga one.",
 },
 {
 q: "Can you colour-match a metallic floor to something specific?",
 a: "Approximately, not exactly, and we would rather set that expectation early. Metallic pigments move during installation - that movement is what creates the effect - so the finished floor is a family of tones rather than a flat colour. We will do a sample board in your chosen palette before install day so you can see the actual range on actual concrete. If you need a precise, uniform colour, a solid-colour polyaspartic is the honest answer rather than a metallic.",
 },
];
---

<Ironside title={title} description={description} heroType="dark">
 <CityServiceSchema
 city={city.name}
 citySlug={city.slug}
 wikidata={city.wikidata}
 serviceType="Epoxy, polyaspartic and metallic floor coating"
 description={description}
 />

 <section class="hero-dark compact">
 <div class="hero-grad"></div><div class="hero-grain"></div><div class="hero-rim"></div>
 <div class="hero-inner">
 <div class="city-hero-grid">
 <div>
 <div class="kicker mb-2">Epoxy, polyaspartic &amp; metallic &middot; Vaughan</div>
 <h1 class="h-hero" style="margin-top:12px;max-width:16ch">Epoxy flooring in Vaughan.</h1>
 <p class="lede" style="color:var(--color-iron-300);margin-top:24px;max-width:56ch">
 Large estate garages, in-floor heat and finishes that are meant to be looked at. Kleinburg,
 Patterson, Vellore Village, Maple and Woodbridge - including the metallic work most
 contractors will not take on.
 </p>
 <div style="display:flex;gap:12px;margin-top:36px;flex-wrap:wrap">
 <a href="#quote" class="btn btn-lg btn-primary">Book a slab assessment</a>
 </div>
 <div class="meta-rule" style="margin-top:36px">
 <span>{city.driveMinutes} min from the Oakville shop via the 407</span><span class="sep"></span>
 <span>Booked as dedicated full days</span>
 </div>
 </div>
 <div id="quote">
 <QuoteForm city={city.name} heading="Free quote in Vaughan" sub="We measure the slab, test it for moisture, and quote a fixed price." />
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

 <section class="section">
 <div class="container">
 <div class="split-64" style="align-items:end">
 <div>
 <div class="kicker">Vaughan garages</div>
 <h2 class="h1 mt-2" style="max-width:22ch">Vaughan garage floors, where the garage is part of the house</h2>
 </div>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);max-width:52ch">
 Vaughan asks for something different from the rest of the region. The garages are bigger,
 more of them are heated and finished, and clients here are far more likely to want a floor
 that is a feature rather than a surface.
 </p>
 </div>

 <div class="hood-grid">
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Kleinburg</h4>
 <p>Estate properties with three and four-bay garages, frequently heated and finished. The bulk of our metallic epoxy work in the region happens here.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Patterson</h4>
 <p>Newer large-format homes, many with in-floor heat in the garage. Young slabs, big footprints, high finish expectations.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Vellore Village</h4>
 <p>2000s subdivision stock at scale. Consistent double garages and predictable slabs - the straightforward half of the Vaughan book.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Woodbridge</h4>
 <p>The widest range of housing ages in the city. Often exceptionally well-maintained slabs, but also the most likely place to find a legacy coating that needs removing.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Maple &amp; Concord</h4>
 <p>A mix of established residential and light industrial. Concord in particular has a good deal of commercial bay work alongside the residential.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

 <section class="section section-sm" style="background:var(--color-iron-50);border-top:1px solid var(--color-iron-100);border-bottom:1px solid var(--color-iron-100)">
 <div class="container">
 <div class="split-64" style="align-items:start;gap:56px">
 <div>
 <div class="kicker">Decorative work</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2" style="max-width:24ch">Metallic epoxy flooring in Vaughan, and when it is wrong</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px;max-width:60ch">
 We install more metallic in Vaughan than in the rest of our service area combined, and we
 also talk more people out of it here than anywhere else. Both facts come from the same
 place: the garages are impressive enough that a feature floor makes sense, and expensive
 enough that getting the specification wrong is a costly disappointment.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px;max-width:60ch">
 Metallic belongs in a heated, finished garage that stays dry - a display bay, a car
 collection, a space that functions as a room. It is a smooth, high-gloss surface, and
 smooth high-gloss surfaces show grit, water spotting and tyre scuffing. Put a
 salt-covered daily driver on one every January and you will spend the winter cleaning it.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px;max-width:60ch">
 The compromise that works well in a multi-bay Vaughan garage is to split the floor:
 metallic in the display bay, full-broadcast flake in the working bays, with a deliberate
 transition line rather than an apologetic one. It costs less than doing the whole floor in
 metallic and it ages far better.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px;max-width:60ch">
 One practical note specific to this market: if your garage has in-floor heat, tell us at
 quote. It needs to be wound down before install and brought back slowly afterwards, and it
 genuinely changes how the day runs.
 </p>
 </div>
 <div>
 <ServiceAreaMap city={city.name} lat={city.lat} lng={city.lng} span={0.13} />
 <div style="margin-top:24px">
 <div class="kicker">Areas we cover</div>
 <div class="nearby">
 {city.neighbourhoods.map((n) => <a href="#quote">{n}</a>)}
 <a href="#quote">Concord</a>
 <a href="#quote">Thornhill</a>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

 <section class="section">
 <div class="container-narrow">
 <div class="kicker">Prep does not change</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2">A Vaughan feature floor still starts with a grinder</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px">
 It is tempting to think that a decorative finish is mostly about the topcoat. It is not. A
 metallic floor is more sensitive to preparation than a flake floor, not less, because there
 are no chips to hide behind - every dip, every trowel mark left in the original pour, every
 patch of unevenly ground surface reads straight through a gloss finish.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 So the sequence is the same on every job we do anywhere: diamond grind to a CSP-2 or CSP-3
 profile, chase and fill cracks with a rigid polyurea, moisture test, then build. No acid
 etching, ever. On a metallic floor we take the grind a stage further and spend longer on
 flatness, because that is what separates a floor that looks like liquid from one that looks
 like paint.
 </p>
 <div class="nearby">
 <a href="/polyaspartic-garage-floors">How polyaspartic differs from epoxy</a>
 <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-cost">What a floor costs</a>
 <a href="/epoxy-flooring">All service areas</a>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>


 <section class="section section-sm" style="border-top:1px solid var(--color-iron-100)">
 <div class="container-narrow">
 <div class="kicker">Working through the seasons</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2">Heated Vaughan garages change the epoxy flooring rules</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px">
 A far higher proportion of Vaughan garages are heated than anywhere else we work, and that
 changes both the seasonal calendar and the technical detail of the install. The obvious
 effect is that winter stops being a constraint at all. The less obvious effect is that a warm
 slab is a faster slab, and faster is not automatically better.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 Polyaspartic is temperature-sensitive in both directions. Cold slows the cure and lengthens
 the working window; warmth accelerates it and shortens the window, sometimes dramatically. On
 a slab sitting at 22&deg;C with in-floor heat running, material that would normally give a
 crew fifteen minutes of working time may give eight. That is manageable if you know about it
 in advance and size the crew accordingly, and it is how floors end up with visible lap lines
 if you do not.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 There is a second effect worth knowing about. Warming a slab drives air out of the concrete
 and up through a wet coating, which can leave pinholes in the finish - small enough to miss
 on install day, obvious under the gloss of a metallic floor once the light hits it. Winding
 the heat down two days before we arrive and bringing it back gradually afterwards avoids
 both problems.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 None of this is a reason to avoid heating your garage. It is a reason to mention it when you
 book, and to be sceptical of anyone who does not ask.
 </p>
 </div>
 </section>

 <section class="section section-sm" style="background:var(--color-iron-50);border-top:1px solid var(--color-iron-100);border-bottom:1px solid var(--color-iron-100)">
 <div class="container-narrow">
 <div class="kicker">Before you sign anything</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2">Judging Vaughan decorative epoxy flooring before it exists</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px">
 Buying a metallic floor is harder than buying a flake floor, because the thing you are buying
 cannot be shown to you in advance. Every metallic install is unique by design, so photographs
 of someone else's garage tell you about a contractor's taste and skill but not about what
 your floor will look like.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 <strong>Ask for a sample board in your palette.</strong> Not a brochure, not a photo - an
 actual piece of coated concrete made with the pigments going into your floor. Any contractor
 doing serious decorative work will offer this without being asked.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 <strong>Ask who is holding the trowel.</strong> Metallic is a hand skill. The person who
 quotes the job is not always the person who installs it, and with decorative work that
 distinction matters far more than it does on a flake floor.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 <strong>Ask about maintenance, honestly.</strong> A gloss metallic floor in a working
 four-bay garage will need more attention than a flake floor. If nobody has mentioned that,
 they are selling rather than advising.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 <strong>Ask what happens at the transitions.</strong> In a multi-bay garage with mixed
 finishes, the line between metallic and flake is the detail everyone notices and few
 contractors plan for. It should be a deliberate design decision made before install day.
 </p>
 </div>
 </section>


 <section class="section section-sm">
 <div class="container-narrow">
 <div class="kicker">Install day</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2">How a Vaughan epoxy floor install actually runs</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px">
 Vaughan is a dedicated-day booking, so the crew arrives with nothing else in the diary. That
 matters on large estate garages where the grind alone can run past midday, and it matters
 even more on decorative work, where rushing the finish is not recoverable.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 Setup is more involved here than elsewhere: these garages are usually finished spaces with
 drywall, trim, cabinetry and sometimes a car lift, all of which needs protecting before a
 grinder starts. If in-floor heat has been wound down as asked, we check the slab temperature
 before mixing anything, because a warm slab shortens the working window and changes how the
 crew is deployed across the floor.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 For a flake floor the sequence is the standard one - basecoat, broadcast to refusal,
 scrape-back, topcoat. For metallic it is different: no broadcast, and instead a poured
 decorative layer worked by hand with trowel and blower while it is still moving, then sealed.
 That stage cannot be hurried and cannot be corrected afterwards, which is the entire argument
 for booking it as its own day.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 Flake work here runs exactly as it does in <a href="/epoxy-flooring-brampton">Brampton</a>
 and <a href="/epoxy-flooring-toronto">Toronto</a>. More on
 <a href="/about">how we work</a>, and the full
 <a href="/service-areas">service-area map</a>.
 </p>
 </div>
 </section>

 <CityFaq faqs={faqs} city={city.name} />

 <section class="section section-sm">
 <div class="container">
 <div class="kicker">Nearby</div>
 <h2 class="h3 mt-2">Epoxy flooring in nearby cities</h2>
 <div class="nearby">
 <a href="/service-areas/oakville">Oakville</a>
 {nearby.map((c) => <a href={`/epoxy-flooring-${c.slug}`}>{c.name}</a>)}
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>
</Ironside>
